  "answer": "Hypothesis — FG-region NUP98 phosphorylation by a TDP-43-dysregulated WEE1–CDC25C/CDK1 axis initiates Ran-gradient collapse before cytoplasmic TDP-43 demixing in TARDBP-mutant motor neurons\n\nIn isogenic human iPSC-derived motor neurons carrying TARDBP M337V, nuclear mutant-TDP-43 dysfunction—assayed before any cytoplasmic assembly—mis-splices the WEE1 and CDC25C transcripts, tilting the CDK1 activation axis. Raised CDK1 activity then phosphorylates NUP98 within its N-terminal FG/GLFG repeat region; that lesion weakens FG cohesion, raises size-selective passive permeability, and collapses the Ran/NTF2-L1 gradient, initiating import failure. Import failure then deepens nuclear TDP-43 depletion, fosters cytoplasmic demixing, and drives dysfunction and death. \"Initiating\" denotes the start of the transport lesion in this TARDBP context, not a universal ALS cause; the C9orf72 repeat-RNA route to RanGAP mislocalization and import failure [S2] is a boundary contrast, not support.\n\nSupported premises. Disease-linked TDP-43 disrupts nucleoporin localization and nuclear transport, with pore pathology in ALS tissue [S1]. Persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies can form independently of conventional stress granules, mislocalize transport-associated proteins, impair import, deplete nuclear TDP-43, and accompany cell death, with phosphorylated TDP-43 accumulating in these assemblies [S3]. Cellular stress can alternatively sequester transport factors into stress granules and impair transport [S4]. No supplied source identifies a nucleoporin phosphorylation event, kinase, residue, or ordering.\n\nSpeculative bridges. Unverified prior knowledge holds that NUP98 FG/GLFG repeats cohere to set passive permeability, that a C-terminal region anchors it at the pore, and that mitotic CDK1 phosphorylation of NUP98 promotes NPC disassembly. Assumed, not evidenced from the packet: (a) partial FG-restricted phosphorylation in postmitotic neurons raises permeability without full disassembly; (b) WEE1 and CDC25C are TDP-43 splicing substrates whose correctly spliced isoforms restrain CDK1, so nuclear TDP-43 dysfunction raises CDK1 occupancy on NUP98; (c) early import failure suffices to drive later TDP-43 redistribution. Whether phosphorylated TDP-43 promotes demixing is untested; [S3] only places it inside assemblies.\n\nDiscriminating preclinical strategy. Use several independently derived M337V iPSC motor-neuron lines and isogenic corrected controls, without exogenous TDP-43 inducer, under one pre-registered maturation clock. Stage 1 grounds the site empirically: enrich native NPCs, run comparative phosphoproteomics across the isogenic pair over a dense sampling grid, and quantify candidate sites by targeted LC-MS/MS (PRM/SRM) against synthetic phospho-peptide standards to set a detection floor. t0 is the first grid window in which mutant import of a releasable NLS reporter diverges from corrected cells by a pre-set effect size; strict precedence requires a qualifying site at least one interval before t0. Site acceptance is pre-registered: detected on endogenous NUP98, mutant-enriched before t0, located inside the FG/GLFG region rather than the anchor domain, and matching a CDK consensus; if no residue qualifies, engineering stops and the hypothesis fails. The upstream bridge carries its own order requirement and occlusion test: the WEE1/CDC25C splicing lesion must precede phosphorylation, and inducible expression of a siRNA-resistant, correctly spliced WEE1 allele before t0 must normalize phosphorylation. Direct kinase logic is verified by purified CDK1 phosphorylating the native NUP98 fragment at that site and by epistasis between analog-sensitive CDK1 inhibition and the site substitution, with no additive rescue expected if phosphorylation is the relevant CDK1 output. Necessity arms introduce endogenous phospho-resistant Ala substitutions and, orthogonally, inducible NUP98-directed phosphatase recruitment (catalytically inactive and untargeted controls), each leaving NUP98 abundance, permeability, and import unchanged in corrected neurons. Sufficiency compares authentic, site-specifically phosphorylated and unmodified NUP98 in a reconstituted FG-cohesion/permeability assay, and in-cell an analog-sensitive CDK1 allele or NUP98-targeted kinase-recruitment module installs and reverses the native modification on demand; the phosphomimetic is retained only as a lower-bound approximation. Concurrent readouts: nuclear-retained wild-type TDP-43 versus a cytoplasmic-restricted control in mutant neurons; independent suppression of cytoplasmic demixing [S3] and of conventional stress-granule assembly [S4]; Ran/NTF2-L1 flux alongside size-resolved passive permeability to separate FG-leakage from active-transport failure; CDK1 versus GSK3β/JNK pore occupancy; and energy plus general-stress markers.\n\nFalsifying observation. The hypothesis fails if: (1) no qualifying FG-region phosphorylation precedes t0 at the validated detection floor; (2) two orthogonal interventions that prevent that phosphorylation while preserving baseline pore function leave the timing and magnitude of import failure, Ran-gradient loss, and neuronal dysfunction unchanged; (3) timed analog-sensitive CDK1 inhibition immediately before t0 fails to abolish the transport lesion; (4) the WEE1/CDC25C splicing lesion appears only after phosphorylation; or (5) nuclear-retained TDP-43 rescue fails to normalize CDK1 occupancy and phosphorylation even when demixing is suppressed. A phosphomimetic that fails the reconstituted assay is inconclusive, not falsifying.\n\nAlternative explanations. Cytoplasmic demixing-first [S3] or stress-granule sequestration-first [S4] ordering; generic proteostatic or metabolic stress coupling phosphorylation and transport together; phosphorylation of the anchor domain or another nucleoporin; and a GSK3β- or JNK-driven stress-kinase account. The C9orf72 route [S2] stays outside this test.\n\nLimitations. Low-stoichiometry sites, redundant kinases, and multiple phosphosites can mask single-site effects; genome editing and phosphatase recruitment can act off-target; NUP98 also mediates mRNA export and chromatin contacts; reconstituted assays and cultured motor neurons omit aging and non-cell-autonomous context. A positive result supports a bounded, TARDBP-specific initiating mechanism, not a validated treatment or universal cause.",
